      bridge: 'Both Nāgārjuna and physics depend on causation being lawful and specific. If non-existent things can cause effects (opponent\'s view), or if vacuum fluctuations are truly random with no constraints (an impossible scenario), all predictability and explanation collapse. Specificity of causation requires conditions, not inherent self-grounding.',
      accessible: 'If an unborn chef can cook dinner, then a half-trained chef can perform brain surgery. Once you allow causation from non-existence, there are no limits. Nāgārjuna\'s ironic "okay, fine" is a devastating rhetorical move — granting the opponent\'s premise destroys their framework.',
      twoTruths: 'Conventionally, only specific causes produce specific effects (seeds produce sprouts, not flowers). The opponent\'s model, by allowing nonarisen things to cause effects, undermines this conventional specificity.',
      commonMisconception: 'Nāgārjuna is NOT agreeing with the opponent. This is irony — a reductio ad absurdum showing that the opponent\'s position, if accepted, leads to the collapse of all meaningful causation.',
